-- title:
--   Igusa function field over k(jmath̄): finite and separable
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $M$ be a non-zero natural number with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, and let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$. Let $w$ be an integral weight-one form of level $M$ over $k$: a modular form of weight $1$ for $\Gamma_1(M)$ (viewed inside $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{R})$) together with a power series over $\mathbb{Z}$ whose image in $\mathbb{C}[[q]]$ is the weight-one $q$-expansion of that form, subject to the requirement that the associated Laurent series $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,k$ obtained by reducing this integral series into $k$ be non-zero. Write $L$ for [`ModularCurve.igusaFunctionFieldX1C k M w`](def/ModularCurve_IgusaFunctionFieldX1.html#L35), the intermediate field of $k((q))$ generated over $k$ by the union of the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC k M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) (the $q$-expansion field of $\Gamma_1(M)$ over $k$) and the single element $w.\mathrm{hasseRootFn} = (\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,k\,w.\mathrm{series})^{-1}$, the inverse in $k((q))$ of that reduced series. The assertion is that there exists an element $t$ of $L$ whose image in $k((q))$ is [`ModularCurve.jqModC k`](def/ModularCurve_JqCoeff.html#L15), namely $q^{-1}$ times the reduction to $k$ of the integral series $E_4^3 \cdot \eta^{-24}$, such that $t$ is transcendental over $k$, and $L$ is finite-dimensional and separable over the subfield $k\langle t\rangle$ of $L$ generated by $t$ over $k$.
--
--   This is the statement that the Igusa function field obtained by adjoining the inverse of a reduced weight-one form to the $q$-expansion field of $\Gamma_1(M)$ in characteristic $p$ is a one-variable function field over $k$, with the reduced $j$-invariant as a separating transcendental element (Katz–Mazur 12.6–12.8). It supplies exactly the input needed to build a smooth proper two-chart model of this field, and is used in the identification of the components of the special fibre of the model of $X(\Gamma_1(M) \cap \Gamma_1(p))$ with Igusa curves. The proof combines the corresponding result for [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L134) with the fact that `w.hasseRootFn` is a Kummer generator of exponent $p-1$ over that field.
